// Practice quiz: Modules: import and export
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/modules-import-and-export

::o1 how many defaults::How many default exports can one module have? {
~One per function #Not quite. The limit is per module.
~As many as you like #Not quite. Named exports can repeat freely with different names, but not defaults.
~Exactly one is required #Not quite. A module may have only named exports.
=At most one #Correct. A second default export is a syntax error.
}

::o1 private by default::A module declares const secret at its top level without exporting it. Another module imports from it. Can the other module read secret? {
~Yes, because top-level names are global #Not quite. That describes classic scripts, not modules.
=No, because module top-level names are private unless exported #Correct. Each module has its own scope.
~Yes, if it uses braces #Not quite. Braces only ask for exported names.
~Only after the first call #Not quite. Timing does not change visibility.
}

::o2 renaming a default::A module has a default export named parse. Which import works? {
~import \{ readValue \} from "./parse.js"; #Not quite. Braces ask for a named export.
~import \{ default \} from "./parse.js"; #Not quite. default is reserved and cannot be a local binding without as.
=import readValue from "./parse.js"; #Correct. A default can be imported under any local name.
~import readValue as parse from "./parse.js"; #Not quite. That form is not valid syntax.
}

::o2 mismatch error::The browser says the requested module does not provide an export named save. What is the likely fix? {
~Add a semicolon after the import #Not quite. Semicolons are not the cause.
~Move the import to the bottom of the file #Not quite. Imports are hoisted; position does not help.
~Change const to let in the exporting file #Not quite. The keyword does not change the export name.
=Match the import form to how save is exported, often removing braces for a default #Correct. Braces only match named exports.
}

::o3 module script::Why does a classic script tag fail on a file that starts with an import statement? {
=import is only valid in module code, so the tag needs type module #Correct. A classic script parses import as a syntax error.
~The browser cannot read files with more than one function #Not quite. File structure is not the issue.
~import only works in Node.js #Not quite. Browsers support it in module scripts.
~It must use the defer attribute #Not quite. defer does not make a script a module.
}
